 1. **Commit in the worktree**: `./zn commit` inside a raft; plain `git add` and `git commit` elsewhere.
 2. **Push and open the PR**: `git push -u origin <branch>`. A raft's PR is opened as a draft by `start`; any other PR is opened ready with `gh pr create`. A capture lands with `./zn land` instead (no PR): it merges `origin/main`, runs the static stages (`z-nile/check.sh --static`) for the projects the capture changed, checks that only the sections, title and `depends_on` of draft backlog issue documents changed, refuses an issue in that diff whose approval no longer matches its contract, pushes one commit to `main` (a lost push retries the push, not the gate) and prints the worktree-removal command. A capture's spend row is committed on the capture branch before `land`, so it is in the tree that push carries.
+   - **Read the PR before every push to it.** Before you take a PR as where the work lives, and again before every push to its branch (`git push`, or a `./zn` command that pushes, such as `start` or `commit`) and every report that it was updated, read its state, head and newest comments: `gh pr view <number or branch> --json state,headRefOid,comments --jq '{state, headRefOid, newest: [.comments[-2:][] | {author: .author.login, createdAt, body}]}'`. An `OPEN` PR takes the push. A `CLOSED` or `MERGED` one is this work's when its head is in the history you push or in origin's tip of the branch: after `git fetch origin refs/pull/<n>/head`, `git merge-base --is-ancestor <headRefOid> HEAD` exits 0, or `git ls-remote --heads origin <branch>` prints a tip and, after `git fetch origin <branch>`, `git merge-base --is-ancestor <headRefOid> FETCH_HEAD` exits 0. This work's closed or merged PR takes nothing: push nothing to its branch, report its state and newest comment (it usually says where the work went), and stop. When each test exits 1 (or origin has no such branch), the PR was an earlier use of the branch's name (z-stop's start fresh), and the branch gets its first push and a new PR, as a branch with no PR does (`gh` answers `no pull requests found`). Any other answer (another `gh` or `git` error) is no answer: retry once, then push nothing and stop. A PR you merged yourself (Merging, or the raft `./zn done` landed) is `MERGED` by design: its post-merge report and step 4 go on, and nothing more is pushed to its branch. A report that the PR was updated quotes the state it read (lesson zz-rules/read-pr-state-before-pushing).
 3. **Merge** a PR only when the conditions under Merging hold. A raft is landed by `./zn done` (one `--no-ff` merge, not a squash) once its review is resolved. Any other PR is merged with `gh pr merge --squash`. A prep the user didn't ask for is not merged without asking, and a capture they didn't ask for is not landed without asking. Under the dispatcher, a step neither asks nor merges: once its PR is open and its spend recorded it ends, and the dispatcher passes the prep and rafting gates itself (the user started it, c0117 G4). (A capture has no PR: `./zn land` in step 2 is its landing.)
 4. **After the merge, one chained command, run last**: `"<root>/zn" sync; git -C <root> worktree remove <path> && git -C <root> branch -D <branch>; git -C <root> worktree prune`. `<root>` is the first path in `git worktree list`; `sync` fast-forwards the root and rebuilds its board. When the session works inside the worktree (with z-work it started in the main checkout and `cd`'d into the worktree), removing it removes the session's own directory, so every command after it must use `-C <root>` or absolute paths, and nothing should run after this command: post the final report first. The alternative is to leave removal to `./zn worktree-prune`, run later from the main checkout.
 
